  "agent_routing": {
    "schema": 1,
    "role": "Routing metadata for Scripts/agent-context.py. Three layers, and the split is the point. domains: keyed by rule id in Scripts/qa-impact-rules.json, which stays the sole owner of source/test/QA mapping; route_role says whether a rule also owns documentation (primary) or only verification (overlay). document_routes: the canonical documentation owner for a set of paths, by glob. A path with a document route takes its owner from there, and every QA rule claiming that path drops to verification for it — which is how one file can be tested by three rules without acquiring three canonical documents, and how a non-behavioural path such as a string table or Scripts/version gets an owner without being given a QA rule it should not have. A document route owns no tests and no QA IDs, ever. An entry id is provenance, not domain identity: owners are grouped by the set of read_first documents they name, so a document route and a QA domain pointing at the same canonical document are one domain and do not escalate. Wherever the manifest already knows a path or a document, these entries point at it with globs_ref/read_first_ref rather than copying it. Routes name canonical project documentation only — agent-specific rule files are the agent's own concern, not the generic router's.",
    "fallback_doc": "knowledge-base/10-ai/AI-INDEX.md",
    "document_routes": [
      {
        "id": "app-localization-tables",
        "name": "App localization tables",
        "globs": [
          "Resources/*.lproj/*.strings",
          "Resources/*.lproj/*.stringsdict"
        ],
        "read_first_ref": "product.localization_doc",
        "reason": "A string table is where most localization work actually starts, and no QA rule owns one: Scripts/qa-impact-rules.json tracks behavioural Swift sources, and adding Resources/*.lproj there to satisfy the router would invent a behavioural owner that does not exist. This route supplies the canonical document and nothing else — no tests, no QA IDs.",
        "conditional": [
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/07-web/website.md",
            "trigger": "a marketing-website locale or route changed — a separate contract from the app"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/07-web/legal-privacy.md",
            "trigger": "a privacy/legal locale, route or policy revision changed — a third, separate contract"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/13-qa/behavioral-qa-matrix.md",
            "trigger": "user-visible behaviour changed"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "version-source",
        "name": "Version source",
        "globs_ref": "product.version_source",
        "read_first_ref": "release.canonical_docs.0",
        "reason": "Scripts/version is the single owner of the version and is release-affecting, but it is not behavioural source, so it has no QA rule and must not be given one. Touching it is release work; the release process document is the owner that says so.",
        "conditional": [
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/13-qa/release-evidence/README.md",
            "trigger": "a version bump needs real manual/hardware QA evidence for that version"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/05-release/release-pipeline.md",
            "trigger": "packaging, signing or the release workflow changed"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/13-qa/behavioral-qa-matrix.md",
            "trigger": "user-visible behaviour changed"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "settings-window",
        "name": "Settings window",
        "globs": [
          "Sources/Impuls/Settings/SettingsWindow.swift"
        ],
        "read_first": [
          "knowledge-base/01-architecture/settings-onboarding-feedback.md"
        ],
        "reason": "One window hosts every setting, so three QA rules claim it: appearance, interface language and version statistics. Two of them are primary domains elsewhere and were therefore making an ordinary Settings edit look like a localization-plus-telemetry cross-domain change. All three still verify this file; none of them documents it. The canonical owner of every surface outside the notch panel is the settings/onboarding/feedback document, and the two specific owners are triggers below.",
        "conditional": [
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/04-development/localization.md",
            "trigger": "the supported-language set or the language picker itself changed"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/07-web/version-statistics-collector.md",
            "trigger": "the version-statistics offer, its consent or its payload changed"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/01-architecture/storage-persistence.md",
            "trigger": "persisted data, its shape or its location changed"
          },
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/13-qa/behavioral-qa-matrix.md",
            "trigger": "user-visible behaviour changed"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "domains": [
      {
        "id": "music-and-web",
        "name": "Music / web player",
        "route_role": "primary",
        "read_first": [
          "knowledge-base/02-modules/music.md"
        ],
        "conditional": [
          {
            "doc": "knowledge-base/01-architecture/networking.md",
            "trigger": "an outbound network path, endpoint or host allow-list changed"
